{
  "_provenance": {
    "status": "SLUGS VERIFIED",
    "verifiedOn": "2026-08-20",
    "method": "Each slug fetched and checked for HTTP 200 plus a .forecast-table__table in the response. Four supplied directly by Brian.",
    "source": "snow-forecast.com",
    "notes": [
      "The per-resort key is a Snow-Forecast URL slug. Coordinates and elevations are no longer stored here — the scraper reads tier elevations off each page.",
      "Palisades is one entry backed by TWO slugs. Snow-Forecast still files the mountain under its former name Squaw-Valley and lists Alpine-Meadows separately; the real resort is the merged, lift-connected operation. Combined per the rule in `combine` below.",
      "Slugs follow no pattern. Taos and Revelstoke are bare; Whistler is Whistler-Blackcomb; Whiteface is Whiteface-Mountain. Never guess one — verify by fetching.",
      "`nws` is a point ON THE MOUNTAIN (mid-slope, not the base town) for api.weather.gov, US resorts only. NWS answers for the ~2.5km grid cell around it; scripts/fetch-weather.js rejects any point whose grid sits below the resort's base elevation, which is what a town forecast looks like. Added 2026-10-28.",
      "The four Cascades resorts (Mt. Baker, Crystal Mountain, Stevens Pass, Mt. Bachelor) were added 2026-10-29 from search results, not fetched: the sandbox cannot reach any of the three sources. Verified by scripts/check-new-resorts.sh on Brian's Mac before the first scrape that includes them. Crystal's OnTheSnow path is crystal-mountain-wa — plain crystal-mountain is the Michigan resort.",
      "Thirteen more added 2026-10-29 at Brian's pick — Tahoe (Sugar Bowl, Northstar, Mt. Rose, Sierra-at-Tahoe, Homewood), the Wasatch (Brighton, Solitude, Snowbasin, Powder Mountain) and Colorado (Vail, Breckenridge, Arapahoe Basin, Wolf Creek). Addresses from search results, checked by check-new-resorts.sh on his Mac before the first scrape. Snowbasin's Snow-Forecast slug is Snow-Basin; Wolf Creek's is Wolf-Creek-Ski-Area (Wolf-Creek-Utah is a different hill).",
      "`tz` (every resort) and `latlon` (the four Canadian resorts, which have no `nws` point) were added 2026-10-29 for the forecast archive: tz turns Snow-Forecast's local AM/PM/night periods into UTC, latlon is where the Open-Meteo ensembles are requested. The Canadian points are approximate mid-mountain coordinates from memory, not fetched; Open-Meteo is also given the Snow-Forecast mid elevation, so a few hundred metres of error in position matters little.",
      "`eccc` (the four Canadian resorts, added 2026-10-31) names the Environment and Climate Change Canada city-page forecast nearest the resort — a town, so a valley forecast: Revelstoke bc-65, Whistler bc-86, Banff ab-49 for Lake Louise, Golden bc-34 for Kicking Horse. Site ids read from the API on Brian's Mac. Alerts are looked up at the resort's own `latlon`; ECCC alert areas are forecast regions tens of kilometres across, so the approximate coordinates are close enough.",
      "Sixteen Canadian resorts added 2026-10-31 at Brian's pick (Fernie, RED Mountain, Whitewater, Panorama, Kimberley, Big White, SilverStar, Sun Peaks, Sunshine Village, Norquay, Marmot Basin, Cypress, Tremblant, Le Massif, Mont-Sainte-Anne, Blue Mountain). Every Snow-Forecast slug was fetched (WebFetch reaches the site; curl does not) and its mid page's title and location checked. Sunshine Village is `Sunshine`, Norquay is `Banff-Norquay`, Le Massif is `Le-Massif-Ski-Area` — the obvious guesses 404. OnTheSnow paths are read off each province's ski-resorts index; Sunshine has two OnTheSnow entries and `sunshine-village` is the one still updated (banff-sunshine last updated in July). `eccc` sites are the nearest town with a city-page forecast: s-codes from dd.weather.gc.ca site_list_en.csv mapped to city codes by ECCC-MSC/msc-pygeoapi resources/wxo_lookup.json — the same lookup reproduces the original four exactly. Fernie uses Sparwood (Cranbrook is ~95 km away), RED uses Trail, Le Massif uses Baie-Saint-Paul, Mont-Sainte-Anne uses Québec, Blue Mountain uses Collingwood. `latlon` is Snow-Forecast's own resort point where its page shows one; Fernie, RED, Panorama and Kimberley are approximate, from memory. Fernie, Panorama and Kimberley keep Mountain time (America/Edmonton) like Kicking Horse; the Quebec and Ontario resorts are America/Toronto."
    ]
  },
  "tier": "mid",
  "combine": {
    "rule": "max",
    "why": "Where a resort has several slugs they are lift-connected faces of one mountain. If one side got 30cm and the other 10cm you ski the side that got it, so snow takes the max. Temp and wind also take the max because the warning thresholds are worst-case by design."
  },
